# The panes scene: a THREE-pane ceiling as assertions
# (docs/multicolumn-plan.md D1/D4/D5). The window declares depth 3 once;
# how many columns fit is the platform's re-decision at every width,
# driven here with real resizes. Positions, not counts: the defect this
# scene exists to catch is a lowering that shows the WRONG panes, and a
# count cannot see it.
#
# WHICH WIDTHS THIS SCENE MAY SAMPLE: the extremes, only. The
# three-pane floors disagree more than the two-pane ones did (Material
# wants 1200dp, GNOME 860sp — 1075px at large text — and WinUI's nest
# wants the outer AND the leftover above 641), so this file samples
# 1400, above every floor, and 360, below every boundary; check-steps
# enforces that band. The MIDDLE rung — which panes shed first when
# only some fit — is where the platforms genuinely differ and no shared
# byte can pin it; each lane's own gate holds its ladder
# (tools/check-pane-ladder.py is the macOS one).
expect_entries 0
expect_title "panes"
expect_panes
# Wide: every pane slot fits. The stack holds only the base root; the
# two empty slots are the platform's own empty state — visible columns,
# but not PANES (D1: a pane is a surface from this window's stack).
resize_window 1400x800
expect_panes "regular/0"
# Push the content entry: it lands in the MIDDLE slot, beside the root.
click button#0
expect_entries 1
expect_panes "regular/0,1"
# Push the detail FROM the content pane: the trailing slot fills and
# every stack surface is on screen at once.
click button#1
expect_entries 2
expect_panes "regular/0,1,2"
# All three AT ONCE, from each platform's REAL tree — the model reads
# above pass for an arm that ran and drew nothing.
expect_ax label#0 "label/root pane"
expect_ax label#1 "label/content pane"
expect_ax label#last "label/detail pane"
# Fully spread, the top covers nothing: the back affordance is absent
# and the verb refuses to drive what the screen does not offer
# (DESIGN.md's back rule).
back
expect_entries 2
expect_panes "regular/0,1,2"
# Narrow: compact, one pane — the deepest. Back now reveals the covered
# content pane, then the root: the collapse is a PRESENTATION of the
# stack, not a second lifecycle.
resize_window 360x600
expect_panes "compact/2"
back
expect_entries 1
expect_panes "compact/1"
back
expect_entries 0
expect_panes "compact/0"
# And back out, because a transition that only works one way is half a
# feature. Only the root remains, so the wide window shows the root
# pane and two empty slots again.
resize_window 1400x800
expect_panes "regular/0"
